Following the measured data for $B_s^0$ decay into $D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$, we evaluate various parametric models. Both shape information from $B^0$ decay into $D_s^{+}s$ and size data from $B_s^0$ decay into $D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ are incorporated using a cluster approach to determine form factor ratios of $\phi_0(q^2)$ and $\xi_0(q^2)$ across different momentum transfers. Our numerical analysis shows that a NLO QCD prediction for size data from $B^0$ decay into $D_s^{+-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ and a LO prediction from an effective vector current model best reflect the underlying physics, with form factor ratios at zero momentum transfer of 0.80(12) and 0.83(13), respectively. Based on both shape and size data, a QCD sum theory model by Colangelo et al. provides the most accurate representation of the physics, with a form factor ratio at zero momentum transfer of 085(13).
